<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Hana</h1><p class="st-meta">1720 BCE – 1350 BCE (approx.) · capital Terqa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hana, a kingdom of the middle Euphrates centred on Terqa, which filled the gap left by Mari's fall and preserved its old traditions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a> c. 1760 BCE, local rulers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terqa">Terqa</a>, some 70 kilometres upstream and long the seat of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>, created the kingdom of Hana. It controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> valley between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> river and the Babylonian frontier.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings, such as Isharlim and Kashtiliashu, issued land contracts in a distinctive Hana style and kept the Mari-era cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>. Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terqa">Tell Ashara</a> found a scribe's house with a jar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a>, which must have travelled from south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Some rulers bore names suggesting links with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hana fell under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire</a> in the 15th century BCE and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>, surviving as a province and a regional name. Its successor on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> was the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/land-of-suhu">Land of Suhu</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1720 BCE – 1350 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:25.47%;width:7.36%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1720 BCE – 1350 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Terqa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Isharlim, Kashtiliashu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Mesopotamian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Akkadian, Amorite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Syria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hana, Terqa</dd></dl></aside></div>
